00:12:41.130 --> 00:12:48.720
made me remember it it stood out in the
pile of material so interesting why do

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:54.000
you think so many whether it's hands got
you know in the pie or why do you think

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:58.920
that it had such a fresh voice and then
it was almost this is just my word but

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:04.470
destroyed maybe that's too strong but
well it's not too strong

00:13:04.470 --> 00:13:13.620
it's the mode of production I think in
at least four big movies is is a problem

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:18.570
I think because when you have a big
movie and this wound up being a big

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:24.840
studio movie I just think there's so
much pressure that it work that there's

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:30.090
defensive things that happen and we need
to make sure that the okay I'm not sure

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:33.990
this draft is right let's get another
writer in and then you know there's

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:40.650
there's so much at stake that it it has
an effect on the product and it can

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:44.190
become incoherent and it is a
collaborative medium and there's always

00:13:44.190 --> 00:13:49.080
going to be even in the best of
circumstances you could have a great

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:54.660
writer director producer casts and and
script and it could still fall flat its

00:13:54.660 --> 00:14:00.810
imprecise but there seems to be the
storytelling tends to flatten out in

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:05.610
these situations because one of the
elements of that I talk about more in

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:09.900
the first book about dramatic irony
dramatic irony is of course Alfred

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:15.930
Hitchcock used it create suspense to
create to use to create a story that

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:21.390
involves withholding some information
from the audience some information from

00:14:21.390 --> 00:14:27.240
the characters when you reveal it that
requires someone who's really watching

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:32.520
it and imagining it in their own mind
the whole time and if you've got a lot

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:36.930
of people involved somebody's gonna go
oh my goodness we sure the audience is

00:14:36.930 --> 00:14:41.880
going to get this well you better make
it clear and if it's clear its risks

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:48.540
being boring the three three three
questions that Frank Danielle shared

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:54.460
with us that I still think apply to any
story he said there's three question

00:14:54.460 --> 00:14:59.620
that you ask when you're crafting a
story 1 what does the main character

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:04.960
want and what are they trying to avoid
hey that's question 1 - what is this the

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:09.010
main character no and what does the main
character not know and then the third

00:15:09.010 --> 00:15:12.960
question is what is the audience know
what is the audience not know and I

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:17.410
don't think most writers realize that
you have control over those second two

00:15:17.410 --> 00:15:21.550
that the audience especially when I work
with students the audience doesn't have

00:15:21.550 --> 00:15:24.910
to know everything all the time and the
characters don't have to know everything

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:31.320
all the time you can withhold some
information you can play games in fact

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:37.060
my takeaway from the book that Connie
and I did the science of screenwriting

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:44.110
see it that came out last year was this
applying constructivist psychology to

00:15:44.110 --> 00:15:51.520
the storytelling process this is
something that was in the 80s a film

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:56.410
critic David Bordwell came out with a
blockbuster scholarly work in which he

00:15:56.410 --> 00:16:03.820
squarely placed storytelling in the
realm of the constructivist psychology

00:16:03.820 --> 00:16:09.160
and that simply means that most of our
experience in life most of our

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:14.950
experience of reality is based not on
knowledge but on inferences that we make

00:16:14.950 --> 00:16:23.710
based on clues so that you have what's
called top-down processing bottom-up

00:16:23.710 --> 00:16:28.300
processing is you see things that goes
into your brain and you make note of it

00:16:28.300 --> 00:16:33.280
and you store information top down is
you see something in the world and your

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:38.020
brain automatically compares what you've
experienced before with what you're

00:16:38.020 --> 00:16:41.530
seeing and then you make a conclusion
you know there's shortcuts that we're

00:16:41.530 --> 00:16:45.250
doing all the time you've never seen the
back of this chair but you've seen

00:16:45.250 --> 00:16:49.690
chairs so you assume the back kind of
looks like any other chair it would be a

00:16:49.690 --> 00:16:54.100
surprise twist at the back of this chair
had a dragon hiding out there you know

00:16:54.100 --> 00:17:00.370
because that's not normal what you're
normally associated with that concept so

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:07.449
in
when you're telling a story you're you

00:17:07.449 --> 00:17:13.179
you if you understand that that's how
audiences are responding their house a

00:17:13.179 --> 00:17:17.589
movie is they're looking for clues and
they're going to put them together and

00:17:17.589 --> 00:17:23.110
they're actively involved in
constructing a reality you're the one as

00:17:23.110 --> 00:17:27.100
a screenwriter as a storyteller you're
giving them the clues you're turning

00:17:27.100 --> 00:17:30.940
them and get in Frank Danielle's term
you're making them the smartest people

00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:35.380
in the world you're making them so
brilliant because they're seeing all

00:17:35.380 --> 00:17:39.549
these clues and you're the one who's
actually giving them to them but they

00:17:39.549 --> 00:17:43.510
think they're figuring it out and
they're gonna try to anticipate where

00:17:43.510 --> 00:17:49.330
you're going because they've seen movies
before the this is an ocean conceptual

00:17:49.330 --> 00:17:53.980
framework called a schema they've seen a
movie they know how movies are they know

00:17:53.980 --> 00:17:57.370
they have tend to have a character who
does this and that and when you know

00:17:57.370 --> 00:18:02.500
that you can play games with them and
that's the for me the most fun thing

00:18:02.500 --> 00:18:08.140
about screenwriting is creating worlds
and driving people crazy getting in

00:18:08.140 --> 00:18:12.940
their heads and you can learn how to get
in their heads just a very simple

00:18:12.940 --> 00:18:18.309
example suppose I show you a movie
there's a shot of a husband and he's

00:18:18.309 --> 00:18:26.440
buying the wife and his wife flowers and
chocolate and an anniversary card okay

00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:33.040
and on his way home from work okay then
meanwhile you see the wife has got a gun

00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:38.790
and she's hiding it in the bedroom
drawer okay what are you gonna think

00:18:38.790 --> 00:18:44.380
well it's pretty obvious isn't it he's
wants to make love and she has other

00:18:44.380 --> 00:18:51.730
plans right okay that's where the
audience is gonna go and that's you

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:54.760
could pay it off that he comes home with
the flowers she pulls out the gun and

00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:59.799
shoots him or you could then disclose
later that the gun that he's gun

00:18:59.799 --> 00:19:04.090
collector and the surprise and this is a
present for him this is a gun he's been

00:19:04.090 --> 00:19:08.620
looking for she saved up for it and she
wants to give it to him for anniversary

00:19:08.620 --> 00:19:15.160
present and then you find out that he
poisoned the chocolate so you we just

00:19:15.160 --> 00:19:19.730
told a
twist is just telling two stories at the

00:19:19.730 --> 00:19:22.850
same time the one the audience thinks
it's seeing and the one is actually seen

00:19:22.850 --> 00:19:28.429
and you're relying when you do that kind
of thing on the audience's propensity to

00:19:28.429 --> 00:19:33.260
figure it out and be smarter than you
and once you've got them going that way

00:19:33.260 --> 00:19:37.880
you can have all kinds of fun so that
that's one element of the constructivist

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:45.020
psychology that that we mentioned in the
book and have examples of the filmmakers

00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:49.190
doing that you know doing that kind of
thing why do two different audience

00:19:49.190 --> 00:19:55.669
viewers see a scene in different ways
well why do they that's again relates to

00:19:55.669 --> 00:20:02.630
constructivist psychology that we all
experience have different experiences of

00:20:02.630 --> 00:20:09.309
the world and we bring different things
to the movie and we're gonna have

00:20:09.309 --> 00:20:14.179
therefore different perceptions based on
